WEBVTT
00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:00.580
<v Ira Glass>Joe Franklin?
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：Joe Franklin？

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.390
<v Joe Franklin>I'm ready.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：我准备好了。

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:02.840
<v Ira Glass>It's Ira Glass here.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：我是Ira Glass。

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.450
<v Joe Franklin>Oh, you're the emcee on the show, Ira.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：哦，你是节目的主持人，Ira。

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.890
<v Ira Glass>I am the emcee on the show. Yes.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：我是节目的主持人。是的。

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.560
<v Joe Franklin>Oh great. Ira? I-R-A, Ira?
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：哦太棒了。Ira？I-R-A，Ira？

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.320
<v Ira Glass>Ira, I-R-A.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：Ira，I-R-A。

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:18.830
<v Joe Franklin>Oh, great. Now hold on one second, Ira. Don't go away. Hello? [UNINTELLIGIBLE]. Call me after 3 o'clock. I have great news for you. Ira.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：哦，太好了。现在等一下，Ira。别走开。喂？[听不清]。3点以后再打给我。我有好消息告诉你。Ira。

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:19.290
<v Ira Glass>Yes.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：好的。

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:33.140
<v Joe Franklin>So listen, Tony. If the phone rings, take it in the back. And then come out and tell me who it is. Just say, Joe's being with a camera crew. Just for about 10 minutes. We'll do about five minutes, 10 minutes, right, Ira?
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：听着，Tony。如果电话响了，去后面接。然后出来告诉我那是谁。就说Joe正在接受摄制组采访。大概10分钟。我们会聊个五到十分钟，对吧，Ira？

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:39.960
<v Ira Glass>That's right.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：没错。

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:54.890
<v Ira Glass>Well, one great thing about starting a new show is utter anonymity. Nobody really knows what to expect from you. This interviewee did not know us from Adam.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：嗯，创办新节目的一大好处就是完全的默默无闻。没人知道会对你有什么期待。这位受访者根本不认识我们。

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:12.160
<v Ira Glass>OK, we're what? About a minute. We're one minute five into the new show. Right now, it is stretching in front of us, a perfect future yet to be fulfilled. An uncharted little world. A little baby coming into the world, no little scars on it or anything.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：好了，我们现在播了多久？大约一分钟。新节目开始一分零五秒了。此刻，完美的未来在前方延展，尚未实现。这是一个未知的世界。像一个刚出生的婴儿，没有任何伤痕。

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:31.130
<v Ira Glass>Nobody hearing my words right now is thinking, "Oh, man, remember that show, back when it used to be good? That show, I never missed that show back in the old days, back in the first couple years before it got so-called popular. Back when it was still good."
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：现在听到我说话的人，没人在想：“天哪，还记得那个节目吗，它以前好的时候？那个节目，我以前从不漏掉，在它还没变得所谓流行之前。在它还很好的时候。”

00:01:31.130 --> 00:02:12.750
<v Ira Glass>No, actually, I think that force, that human desire to say that is so strong, to say that "I was there back when that show was good," that force is so strong, it is so basic to who we are as people that I know-- OK, what are we? We are two minutes into the program-- I know that somewhere out there, one or two of you are saying, "Oh, sure. I used to listen to that show back in the first 30 seconds, back when it used to be really good. Remember back when they used to do all that crazy stuff? When they had that guy on the phone? Remember back then?"
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：不，实际上，我认为那种力量，那种想要说出“我见证过它辉煌时期”的人类欲望太强烈了，这种力量太基础了，所以我知道——好了，我们播了多久？节目开始两分钟了——我知道在某些地方，你们中的一两个人肯定在说：“哦，当然。我以前就在最初那30秒听过那个节目，在它真的很好的时候。还记得那时候他们做那些疯狂事情的时候吗？当他们让那个人打电话来的时候？还记得那时候吗？”

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:35.340
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ, in the glorious city of Chicago, Illinois. The name of this show is Your Radio Playhouse. I'm your emcee. I'm your emcee, Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：那么，来自伊利诺伊州芝加哥市的WBEZ电台。这个节目的名字是《Your Radio Playhouse》。我是你们的主持人。我是你们的主持人，Ira Glass。

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:52.380
<v Ira Glass>OK, the idea of this show, this new little show, is stories, some by journalists and documentary producers, like myself, some just regular people telling their own little stories, some by artists, and writers, and performers of all different kinds. And the idea is we're going to bring you stuff you're not going to find anywhere else. And there is also going to be music.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：好的，这个新节目的构思是讲述故事，有些是由像我一样的记者和纪录片制作人带来的，有些是普通人讲述他们自己的小故事，还有些是由各类艺术家、作家和表演者讲述的。我们的想法是为你带来你在其他地方找不到的内容。当然也会有音乐。

00:02:52.380 --> 00:03:22.100
<v Ira Glass>And tonight's show, we thought that we would have a theme. Tonight's show is going to be New Beginnings. And to kick things off, I called the man who's had, as best as anybody can tell, the longest running program in the history of television. His name is Joe Franklin, and his program ran for 43 years on local television in New York. And he claims that he invented the talk show format. And I called him to get some advice on how to create a long-running, healthy program.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：今天的节目，我们想设定一个主题。今晚的节目主题是“新的开始”。为了拉开序幕，我打电话给了一位可以说是电视史上拥有最长寿节目的人。他的名字是Joe Franklin，他的节目在纽约当地电视台播出了43年。他声称他发明了谈话节目形式。我打电话给他，是为了请教如何创办一个长期且健康的节目。

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:40.150
<v Joe Franklin>I've been called in many times to give a sort of a Dean, the elder statesman, even though I'm still a young kid. But I've been called in to give this kind of advice to new kids on the block. Conan O'Brien had me on his first show. And people like that. There's no guidance. It's a matter of paying attention.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：我经常被请去发表一些前辈式的见解，尽管我还是个小伙子。但我经常被请去给新人提建议。Conan O'Brien上过我的第一个节目。像那样的人。没有所谓的指导。关键是要留心。

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:59.070
<v Joe Franklin>Your voice, I've heard so much about the sparkle, about the energy in your voice. The voice, on radio especially, is everything. And when the guest is sitting with you, you've got to look into his eyes. Many times, you get an author on there who's begrudgingly sitting there. He'd rather be home in his ivory tower.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：你的声音，我听说了很多关于你声音中的火花和能量。声音，特别是在广播里，就是一切。当嘉宾坐在你对面时，你必须注视他的眼睛。很多时候，你会遇到一个不情愿坐在那里的作者。他宁愿待在他象牙塔里的家里。

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:27.780
<v Joe Franklin>And above all, get the plug fast. Otherwise, he's worried you're not going to make the plug for the book. And I created a line, Ira, that's been picked up by George Burns. I always said, the main ingredient for longevity in the talk show field, where the mortality rate is so staggering, the main ingredient is sincerity. And once you learn to fake that, then you got it made. I made that up. And I just played it by ear. I was a natural born talker, I guess.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：最重要的是，赶紧让他做宣传。否则，他会担心你没能为他的书做宣传。我创造了一句台词，Ira，后来被George Burns引用了。我总是说，谈话节目领域长寿的主要秘诀——这个领域的死亡率高得惊人——主要秘诀就是真诚。一旦你学会了伪装真诚，你就成功了。那是我编的。我只是随机应变。我想我是一个天生的演说家。

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:36.310
<v Ira Glass>So let me just summarize what I'm getting from you. You're saying you should just pay attention, look people in the eye, be sincere when I'm on the air, get in the plug early.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：那让我总结一下我从你这儿得到的信息。你是说应该多留心，看着人们的眼睛，直播时要真诚，尽早进行宣传。

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:45.480
<v Joe Franklin>That's the key. And don't look in their nose or their belly button. Look right in the eye. Eye contact is everything. And as I say, it's a lot of fun.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：那才是关键。而且不要盯着他们的鼻子或肚脐。要直视眼睛。眼神交流就是一切。正如我所说，这很有趣。

00:04:45.480 --> 00:05:04.860
<v Joe Franklin>And I never called anybody in my life to come on to my show. And I'm sure that they'll be coming to you too. I heard about you, and I called you and wanted to wish you good look, Ira. Imagine the thrill of John F. Kennedy walking into my studio. Richard Nixon. Ronald Reagan, five times. And Bing Crosby.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：我这辈子从没请过任何人上我的节目。我相信他们也会主动来找你。我听说过你，所以我打电话给你，想祝你好运，Ira。想象一下John F. Kennedy走进我演播室的激动时刻。Richard Nixon。Ronald Reagan，五次。还有Bing Crosby。

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:14.360
<v Joe Franklin>And I've got a major book out right now, by the way, Ira, called Up Late with Joe Franklin from Simon & Schuster. And if you find a copy in the store--
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：顺便提一下，Ira，我现在有一本重要的书出版了，叫《Up Late with Joe Franklin》，由Simon & Schuster出版。如果你在店里看到一本——

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:19.080
<v Ira Glass>You see now, I guess I've sort of messed that one up, because I didn't let you get in your plug early.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：你看，我想我把那件事搞砸了，因为我没让你尽早做宣传。

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:34.590
<v Joe Franklin>See, that is my fault. I got it in toward the bitter end. It's like Duke Ellington. He always used to have his dessert at the beginning of the meal. Duke Ellington said, because he would never have no room for it at the end. So he had his dessert at the beginning. Who is it? Well, no, good news, 6 o'clock.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：看，那是我的错。我在最后关头才说出来。这就像Duke Ellington。他总是先吃甜点。Duke Ellington说，因为他最后吃不下。所以他先吃甜点。是谁啊？哦，不，好消息，6点钟。

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.740
<v Ira Glass>Well, Joe Franklin, thank you very much for being with us on our premier show.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：好了，Joe Franklin，非常感谢你参加我们的首播节目。

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.490
<v Joe Franklin>Ira, I'm going to be a listener and a fan. And let's always be in touch.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：Ira，我会成为你的听众和粉丝的。让我们保持联系。

00:05:40.490 --> 00:06:10.920
<v Joe Franklin>[MUSIC - "DESTINATION MOON" BY DINAH WASHINGTON]
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：[音乐 - Dinah Washington的“DESTINATION MOON”]

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:16.820
<v Ira Glass>All right, Your Radio Playhouse. All right. I'm making eye contact with you right-- wait, how does Joe put it? Wait a second.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：好了，《Your Radio Playhouse》。好了。我正与你进行眼神交流——等等，Joe是怎么说的？等一下。

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:19.330
<v Joe Franklin>Look right in the eye. Eye contact is everything.
<v Joe Franklin>Joe Franklin：直视眼睛。眼神交流就是一切。

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:41.600
<v Ira Glass>I am making eye contact with you right now. That is just how much I have already learned. OK, so the thing about new beginnings is that there are the ones that we actually undertake and then there are the ones that we just wish for. And the ones that we wish for pretty much outnumber the ones that we really undertake.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：我现在正与你进行眼神交流。我学到的就是这些。好的，关于新的开始，有些是我们真正着手去做的，而有些是我们只是在心里祈求的。而那些我们心里祈求的，数量远多于我们真正着手去做的。

00:06:41.600 --> 00:07:08.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, Kevin Kelly spent most of his twenties wandering around Asia. He was basically wandering around as a freelance photographer. And he found himself photographing a lot of religious ceremonies and drawn to religious ceremonies of all sorts. And he says that he was really confused about what he believed. And he was the kind of person who had always dreamt about a new beginning, where he wouldn't struggle with these questions.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：Kevin Kelly在他二十多岁时大部分时间都在亚洲游荡。他基本上是以自由摄影师的身份四处游荡。他发现自己拍摄了很多宗教仪式，并被各种各样的宗教仪式所吸引。他说他对自己的信仰感到非常困惑。他一直梦想着一个新的开始，在那里他不必为这些问题而挣扎。

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:39.450
<v Kevin Kelly>I would get twisted and caught up. And these things were in the background, consuming me. And actually, I found that I could think about little else for many, many months, that behind all that I was doing, there was always this unresolved question of was God real. And if he was real, then how could we ignore him? And if we were trying to not ignore him, what would we do? And if he was real, then what about these other things that people said about God?
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我会变得纠结和深陷其中。这些问题一直在背景中折磨着我。实际上，我发现好几个月里我几乎无法思考其他事情，我所做的一切背后，始终都有这个未解的疑问：上帝是真的吗？如果他是真的，那我们怎么能忽视他？如果我们试图不去忽视他，我们该怎么做？如果他是真的，那人们关于上帝的其他说法又是怎么回事？

00:07:39.450 --> 00:08:05.950
<v Ira Glass>We will not attempt to answer these questions, by the way, in this hour. I just want to just give you a sense of scope here, just modest scope. But what we want to talk about is what happened to Kevin Kelly. What happened is that at the age of 27, all of this changed when he came into Jerusalem on the eve of Easter and Passover. It was the same weekend. And flocks of people are coming into the city.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：顺便说一下，在这个小时里，我们不会试图回答这些问题。我只是想让你感受一下主题的范围，适度的范围。但我们要谈论的是Kevin Kelly身上发生了什么。事情是这样的，27岁那年，当他在复活节和逾越节前夕来到耶路撒冷时，一切都改变了。那是同一个周末。成群结队的人涌入城市。

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:43.520
<v Kevin Kelly>So I entered Jerusalem on Easter with a simple expectation that I was going to photograph another religious ceremony, another religious festival. And then, for various reasons, I got locked out of my hostel room. They had a curfew. And I didn't make it back in time. And I was in quite a fix because I was a stranger in this very strange town. When it happened, I didn't have enough money to stay elsewhere, nor did I even have knowledge of where to go.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：所以我在复活节那天进入耶路撒冷，简单地期待着拍摄另一个宗教仪式，另一个宗教节日。然后，由于各种原因，我的旅舍房间被锁住了。他们实行宵禁。我没能及时赶回。我陷入了困境，因为我是在这个极其陌生的城市里的陌生人。发生的时候，我没有足够的钱住在别处，甚至不知道该去哪里。

00:08:43.520 --> 00:09:40.940
<v Kevin Kelly>So I wandered the Old Town of Jerusalem at night, which had been shuttered up and was like a time machine. It was as if I had been transported back to the 15th century, because all the souvenir vendors were gone, and what was left were the labyrinthian paths of cobbled passageways. And I wandered around for a number of hours, and it was getting colder. Eventually, I found myself at the one place that was still open, which was some of the churches. And particularly, after wandering around until about 2:00 or 3:00 in the morning, I finally settled into the Church of the Holy Scepter, which is called and viewed as the church built over the mound where Jesus Christ was crucified.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：所以我晚上游荡在耶路撒冷老城，它已经关门了，就像一台时间机器。仿佛我被传送回了15世纪，所有的纪念品商贩都消失了，剩下的是布满鹅卵石的迷宫般的通道。我游荡了几个小时，天气越来越冷。最后，我发现自己来到了唯一还开放的地方，也就是一些教堂。特别是，在凌晨两三点左右游荡之后，我终于在圣墓教堂安顿下来，也就是被视为建在耶稣基督被钉死在十字架上的土丘之上的教堂。

00:09:40.940 --> 00:10:27.350
<v Kevin Kelly>And I was getting very tired. And there weren't many people around. And so eventually, I laid myself out on about the only flat area that was left, which was this marble slab underneath some pendants that had incense on them. And this was presumably the slab that commemorated the exact positioning of the crosses. So I slept there. I slept on the crucifixion spot on that night because it was the only place-- they had no place in the inn.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我非常累。周围没有太多人。于是最后，我躺在了唯一剩下的平坦区域上，也就是一些挂着香炉的吊灯下的一块大理石板。据推测，这块石板纪念的是十字架的确切位置。所以我睡在那里。那天晚上我睡在钉死十字架的地方，因为没有别的地方可去，旅店没有空房。

00:10:27.350 --> 00:11:44.270
<v Kevin Kelly>I slept there until early morning, when the activity started to increase, and people started coming in. And I went out and followed the crowd where it was going when they were going out to the tombs area in Jerusalem. And I went out. And there were some folding chairs set up in front of this tomb area. And as the sun was coming up on that Easter morning, I was staring at empty tombs. And for a reason that I can not comprehend, as I sat on that chair contemplating this view of the early sun morning coming into the empty tombs, all that I had been wrestling with for the past many, many years in thinking about religion sort of became resolved in my mind. And at that very moment, I believed that Jesus Christ had, indeed, risen from those tombs.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我一直睡到清晨，活动开始增多，人们开始涌入。我走出去，跟着人群走向耶路撒冷的墓地。我出去了。墓地前放着一些折叠椅。当复活节早晨太阳升起时，我盯着空荡荡的坟墓。出于某种我无法理解的原因，当我坐在椅子上凝视着晨光照进空坟墓的景象时，过去许多年来我一直在思考的关于宗教的所有挣扎都在心中得到了解决。那一刻，我相信耶稣基督确实从那些坟墓中复活了。

00:11:44.270 --> 00:12:14.140
<v Kevin Kelly>In an instant, the tension of trying to figure things out was resolved, because now, suddenly, everything was figured out. It was as if you had been working on a problem for a long time and suddenly the answer was there. And it was very clear that was the answer. And although there were many things that were still not clear to you, you were very certain that you were on the right path.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：刹那间，试图弄清楚事情真相的紧张感消失了，因为现在，一切都清楚了。仿佛你一直在研究一个难题，突然答案就出现了。而且很清楚那就是答案。虽然有很多事情你仍然不明白，但你非常确定自己走在正确的道路上。

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:32.120
<v Kevin Kelly>Having that realization that I believe that Jesus Christ had actually risen from those tombs did not settle 1,001 other things about what one was supposed to do with that, what I was supposed to do with that. Did that mean I was supposed to be a monk? Did that mean I was supposed to be an evangelist?
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：意识到我相信耶稣基督真的从那些坟墓中复活了，并没有解决关于一个人应该如何利用这个事实的其他一千零一个问题。这意味着我应该成为一名僧侣吗？这意味着我应该成为一名传道者吗？

00:12:32.120 --> 00:13:35.720
<v Kevin Kelly>Did that mean that I had to immediately renounce all that I had, and get into sackcloths and ashes, and march out into the desert? All that was left unopened. And that is, in fact, what occupied my mind as I went back to my hostel to lay down and think about. Because I had no clue what it really meant to me ultimately, and that's what I was pondering when I was laying there napping. And I wouldn't say it was a voice, but there was an idea that came into my mind that just would not go away, and that was that I should live as if I would die in six months, that I should really, truly live. And that I could not tell for certain whether I would really die, but that either way, I should live as if I was going to die. And so that was the assignment.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：这意味着我必须立即放弃我拥有的一切，穿上麻衣灰烬，走进沙漠吗？所有这些问题都悬而未决。而这正是当我回到旅舍躺下思考时占据我大脑的内容。因为我根本不知道这对我是什么终极意义，这就是我小憩时琢磨的事情。我不会说那是某种声音，但有一个念头出现在我的脑海里，挥之不去，那就是我应该像只剩下六个月生命那样活着，我应该真正地、切实地活着。我不确定我是否真的会死，但无论如何，我都应该像我就要死了一样活着。这就是那个任务。

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:57.530
<v Kevin Kelly>I'm a pretty rational person. I'm pretty logical. And after thinking the thought that I should live as if I was going to die in six months, the first thought that comes to my head was, "Well, that's pretty silly. I have no evidence whatsoever. I could live like I'm going to die in six months and not die at all. It would just be an interesting exercise."
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我是一个相当理性的人。我相当合乎逻辑。在想到我应该像六个月后就要死那样活着之后，我脑子里出现的第一个念头是：“嗯，那太傻了。我没有任何证据。我可能像六个月后就要死那样活着，结果却根本没死。那只会是一个有趣的练习。”

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:34.910
<v Kevin Kelly>But at the same time, it was equally probable that I might die in six months. It happened all the time. There was no guarantee that I wouldn't die. And so fairly quickly, I decided that I could not settle that issue of whether I would really die or not, or just think that I was going to die in six months, and that, in either case, the important thing was to live as if I really believed that I was going to die in six months, which is what I set out to do.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：但与此同时，我也很有可能在六个月后死去。这种事随时都在发生。没有任何保证我不会死。所以很快，我决定我无法解决关于我是否真的会死的问题，或者只是认为我六个月后会死，无论哪种情况，重要的是像我真的相信自己六个月后会死那样生活，这就是我着手去做的事情。

00:14:34.910 --> 00:15:43.690
<v Kevin Kelly>The next couple days, I had the joyous experience of saying to myself, "OK, what do I do for six months if I have only six months to live?" And the answers to that surprised me as much as the assignment, because after thinking that through and contemplating it, the conclusion that I came to was that what I wanted to do for six months was to go home and be ordinary, to go back to my parents, to help them take out the trash, and trim the hedges, and move furniture around, and to be with them. And I was really shocked by that, because I thought that with six months to live, I would climb Mount Everest, or I would go scuba diving to the depths of the ocean, or get in a speedboat and see how fast I could go. But instead, I wanted to go back home and be with my family for that time.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：接下来的几天，我有一种快乐的经历，我对自己说：“好了，如果我只有六个月的时间，我该做什么？”这个问题的答案让我像惊讶于那个任务本身一样感到惊讶，因为深思熟虑之后，我得出的结论是，我想做的事情是回家过平凡的生活，回到父母身边，帮他们倒垃圾，修剪树篱，移动家具，和他们在一起。我对此感到非常震惊，因为我以为如果只剩六个月生命，我会去爬珠穆朗玛峰，或者去深海潜水，或者坐上快艇看看我能开多快。但相反，我只想回家，在那段时间里和家人在一起。

00:15:43.690 --> 00:16:15.270
<v Kevin Kelly>I, of course, did not tell anybody my crazy idea. This is, in fact, the first time I'm really talking about it publicly. Because it's a very scary and alarming idea. And I never told anybody why I was coming home.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：当然，我没有告诉任何人我这个疯狂的想法。事实上，这是我第一次真正公开谈论它。因为它是一个非常可怕和惊人的想法。我从没告诉过任何人我为什么回家。

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:48.840
<v Kevin Kelly>I got back to where my parents live in New Jersey, and things were unbelievably ordinary. And yet, I found myself relishing the ordinariness and finding it in some ways as exotic as anything that I had traveled to see. I helped around the house. I dug up shrubs. I worked on a deck. I moved furniture, washed dishes.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我回到了父母居住的新泽西州，一切都不可思议地平凡。然而，我发现自己很享受这种平凡，在某种程度上，我觉得它和我去过任何地方一样充满异国情调。我帮忙打理家务。我挖掉灌木。我修理甲板。我移动家具，洗碗。

00:16:48.840 --> 00:17:30.290
<v Kevin Kelly>And I was intending to spend my last remaining six months at home getting to know my parents better and myself, hopefully. But about three months into that, my travel urges, I guess, got the better of me. And what I was most concerned about was I wanted to see my brothers and sisters. I had four brothers and sisters. And they were scattered all across the country. And so I felt very strongly that I wanted to see them before I died. And I got the idea that the way to see them was to ride my bicycle across the country and visit them on bicycle.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我打算在家里度过我生命中最后的六个月，更好地了解我的父母，希望也能了解自己。但大约三个月后，我的旅行欲望战胜了我。我最担心的是我想见我的兄弟姐妹。我有四个兄弟姐妹。他们散布在全国各地。所以我强烈地感到在死前想见他们一面。我想到可以通过骑自行车横跨全国的方式去拜访他们。

00:17:30.290 --> 00:18:21.090
<v Kevin Kelly>But before I did that, I made up a will to dispose of the little things that I had. And I had some money left over. And one of the things I did with that money was I went to the bank and got some cashier's checks for $500 and $1,000. And I mailed the money to various people anonymously as gifts. And I think giving away those thousands of dollars was the first true act of charity I had ever done. Because there was absolutely no way for any kind of gratitude or elevated feelings to come back to me, because the people had no idea who had sent them that money.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：但在那之前，我立了一份遗嘱来处理我拥有的小物件。我剩下了一些钱。我用这些钱做的一件事是去银行取了一些500美元和1000美元的本票。我匿名把这些钱作为礼物寄给了不同的人。我认为捐出这些数千美元是我做过的第一次真正的慈善行为。因为没有任何感激之情或高尚的情绪能回到我身上，因为那些人根本不知道是谁寄给他们的钱。

00:18:21.090 --> 00:18:57.160
<v Kevin Kelly>It was really remarkable to see the consequences of getting an anonymous gift like that. Because when you get a check for $1,000 in the mail, you immediately become suspicious of all your friends of having given that to you. And so there's this suspicion of charity, suspicion of goodness that starts to infect the people that are around you. And you look at someone, you think, "Hm, I wonder if he gave me that $1,000?"
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：看到这种匿名馈赠的结果真的很了不起。因为当你收到一张1000美元的支票时，你立刻会怀疑是你所有的朋友送给你的。所以这种对慈善的怀疑、对善意的怀疑开始感染你周围的人。你看着某个人，心想：“嗯，不知道是不是他给了我那1000美元？”

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:15.830
<v Ira Glass>Does that make sense? You look at them, and you think, "I wonder if he gave me that $1,000?" And then you act really nice to him. And then the next person you see, the next of your friends, you think, "Could this be the person?" And then you act really nice to them.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：那有道理吗？你看着他们，心想：“不知道是不是他给了我那1000美元？”然后你对他表现得非常好。接着你看到的下一个人，你的下一个朋友，你心想：“会不会是他？”然后你对他表现得非常好。

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:37.570
<v Ira Glass>I almost want to begin a little speech here about let us all now take up this practice. All of us. Everyone within the sound of my voice. If we all could just do this right now, then I would believe that our little radio show, just 19 minutes into the program, had contributed in some way.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：我几乎想在这里开始一个小演讲，呼吁我们大家都来实践这个做法。我们所有人。在听到我声音的每一个人。如果我们都能现在就做，那么我会相信我们的广播节目，仅仅播出了19分钟，就已经在某种程度上做出了贡献。

00:19:37.570 --> 00:20:31.720
<v Kevin Kelly>I had enough money left over to basically pay for food and whatnot on my bicycle journey across America. And the path that I had to visit all my brothers and sisters was not a direct route, going from San Francisco to New York. I actually had to go up to Idaho, and back down to Texas, and then back up through Indiana. So it was a 5,000 mile trip. The day which, coincidentally, was exactly six months from when I had this assignment, was October 31. It was Halloween. And so the plan would be that I would ride back home, so that I would come back to die on the day after Halloween.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我剩下的钱基本上够支付我横跨美国的自行车旅行的食物和必需品。为了拜访我所有的兄弟姐妹，我走的路线不是直接从旧金山到纽约的。我实际上必须去爱达荷州，再回到德克萨斯州，然后再穿过印第安纳州。所以这是一次5000英里的旅行。那天，巧合的是，距离我接受任务正好六个月，是10月31日。那是万圣节。所以计划是我骑车回家，在万圣节后的第二天死去。

00:20:31.720 --> 00:21:08.310
<v Kevin Kelly>I think there are a lot of people who have trouble staying in the present. There are some people who like to slip into the past as a means to perhaps fantasize or escape. And they find that the past is the place that they retreat to. And I often retreat to the future. I was not a person who planned or had a career staged out, or who had a particular woman he wanted to marry some day, or some vision of a house. The future that I found so hard to give up was a much more insidious type.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我认为很多人都难以活在当下。有些人喜欢逃避到过去，以此作为幻想或逃避的手段。他们发现过去是一个可以退缩的地方。而我经常退缩到未来。我不是那种会做规划或制定职业生涯的人，也没有一个想在某天娶的特定女人，或者对房子的某种愿景。我发现很难放弃的未来是一种更加隐蔽的类型。

00:21:08.310 --> 00:22:04.730
<v Kevin Kelly>It was that of I'd like to buy this record because, in the future, I want to hear this song again and again. Or I will read this book, and there are some cool ideas in it because someday I may write an article about this. And it's good to know that. There was a sense in which my entire life was shifted to the future. And the thought of doing something now for the enjoyment, or the pleasures, or the principle of the function of just right now, without any sense at all that it would ever be used again or that it could ever be brought forward, was extremely difficult and disconcerting. And I fought it day by day and tooth by tooth.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：那就是“我想买这张唱片，因为将来我想一次又一次地听到这首歌”。或者“我会读这本书，里面有一些很酷的创意，因为有一天我可能会写一篇关于这个的文章”。这很好，我知道。从某种意义上说，我的整个人生都被转移到了未来。而那种为了享受、乐趣或仅仅是为了当下的功能而做某事，而不觉得它以后会被再次使用，或者它能被带入未来的想法，是极其困难和令人不安的。我一天天地、竭尽全力地与这种心态做斗争。

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:30.060
<v Kevin Kelly>One of the ways I dealt with this was that I was actually able, by the last weeks, to not think about my life beyond Halloween. There was a way which I had just-- each time a thought came up about something that was beyond this horizon, I just said, "Nope, can't think about it. It doesn't work. We have to dwell in the present."
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我应对这种心态的方法之一是，在最后几周，我实际上能够不去想万圣节之后的生活。每当有一个关于超越这个时间范围的想法出现时，我就对自己说：“不，不能想它。那没用。我们必须活在当下。”

00:22:30.060 --> 00:23:16.660
<v Kevin Kelly>And at the same time I was doing that, and I was able to do that, I also decided that it was an entirely unnatural and inhumane way to live. And that having a future is part of what being human is about. And that when you take away the future for humans, you take away a lot of their humanness. And that it's not actually a very good thing to live entirely in the present. That one needs to have a past, and one needs to have a future to be fully human.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：与此同时，在我这样做的时候，我同时也认为这是一种完全不自然和不人道的生活方式。拥有未来是身为人类的一部分。当你剥夺了人类的未来，你就剥夺了他们很大一部分人性。完全活在当下并不是一件真正好的事情。一个人需要拥有过去，也需要拥有未来才能成为完整的人。

00:23:16.660 --> 00:24:00.090
<v Kevin Kelly>It was a journey that began at the tomb of Jesus. And as I set off to my own presumed death, I did, indeed, think about Jesus Christ who, according to the Gospels, surrendered his own life in a very knowing way. So we have the history in the Gospels of Jesus's torment in his soul, as he approached what he knew of his anointed time to die. So it was, again, that very harsh information of knowing when you're going to die.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：那是一段始于耶稣坟墓的旅程。当我踏上我预期的死亡之旅时，我确实想到了耶稣基督，根据福音书，他以一种非常知情的方式交出了自己的生命。所以我们有福音书中耶稣灵魂受折磨的历史，当他接近他所知的受膏时刻的死亡时。所以，这又是那种知道自己何时会死的极其严酷的信息。

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:41.350
<v Kevin Kelly>And Jesus's soul was in great turmoil and pain because of knowing that. And I think I did experience some of that, not because I had the same weight. It was just my own life. But Jesus prayed that this burden be lifted, and there were days when I did pray that, that if I didn't have to die, I really would rather not.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：耶稣的灵魂因为知道这一点而陷入了极大的动荡和痛苦。我想我确实经历了一些，不是因为我有同样的沉重感。那只是我自己的生活。但耶稣祈求这负担被解除，确实有几天我也这样祈求，如果我不必死，我真的宁愿不死。

00:24:41.350 --> 00:25:14.230
<v Kevin Kelly>By late fall, I was pedaling through the Appalachians, and it was getting colder and colder. And my hands were freezing on the bicycles, and there was ice on my tents in the morning when I got up. And as each day went on, I was coming closer and closer to terrain that I was familiar with and that felt like home. And I was riding into New Jersey, and I was elated. I was elated that I had accomplished this long journey. And I was elated that I was home to see my parents.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：到了深秋，我骑车穿过阿巴拉契亚山脉，天气越来越冷。我的手在自行车上冻僵了，我早上起床时帐篷上有冰。随着每一天的推移，我越来越接近我熟悉的、感觉像家一样的地方。我骑车进入新泽西州，我欣喜若狂。我为自己完成了这段漫长的旅程而欣喜若狂。我为回到家见到父母而欣喜若狂。

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:39.930
<v Kevin Kelly>And I came in to their house on Halloween day. And I was so filled with ideas, and things, and emotions, that I didn't really say very much. And again, I couldn't say very much. I think we had a wonderful dinner. They were, of course, glad to see me because they hadn't seen me in a long time. They knew I was coming back, and we had a wonderful dinner.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我在万圣节那天进入了他们的家。我心中充满了想法、事物和情感，我真的没说太多。而且，我也没法说太多。我想我们吃了一顿美妙的晚餐。他们当然很高兴见到我，因为他们很久没见到我了。他们知道我会回来，我们共进了一顿美妙的晚餐。

00:25:39.930 --> 00:26:17.330
<v Kevin Kelly>We had baskets of candy, which I gave out to the kids. And we had a discussion that night which was about nothing in particular. It was not about the future. It was just about, I think, talking about our family and my brothers and sisters. And I was telling them all that I  had learned about them. And so it was a very together and, again, not a very dramatic evening, but just a pleasant one, one that you might have a memory about as you were dying, which was not a special evening, but just an ordinary evening.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：我们有篮子里的糖果，我发给了孩子们。那天晚上我们进行了一次讨论，内容并不具体。不是关于未来。我想只是关于我们的家庭和我的兄弟姐妹。我告诉了他们我所了解的关于他们的一切。这是一个非常亲密、同样不是非常戏剧性，但却很愉快的夜晚，一个你可能会在临终时回忆起的夜晚，那不是一个特别的夜晚，只是一个平凡的夜晚。

00:26:17.330 --> 00:26:50.230
<v Kevin Kelly>And I went to bed that night, which was a very difficult thing to do because I was fully prepared at that point never to wake up again. I had been praying. I had gotten everything arranged. I had fully gone through in my own mind, in my own soul, all the things that I might have regretted. And I had righted as many of those as I thought I could through letters. And I was prepared, as much as anybody could be prepared to die.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：那天晚上我去睡觉了，这是一件非常困难的事情，因为那时我已经完全做好了永远不再醒来的准备。我一直在祈祷。我已经安排好了一切。我已经在我的脑海中、在我的灵魂中，彻底审视了我可能后悔的所有事情。我尽可能通过信件纠正了其中的大部分。我已经像任何人可能做到的那样，做好了死亡的准备。

00:26:50.230 --> 00:27:39.830
<v Kevin Kelly>And so I went to bed while the kids were still ringing doorbells. And I went to sleep, because I was very tired after that long trip. And I didn't know what was going to happen the next day. I thought I had done all that I could. And the next morning, I woke up. And the next morning, I woke up, and it was as if-- The next morning I woke up, and it was as if I had the entire-- my entire life again.
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：于是，当孩子们还在按门铃时，我就去睡觉了。我入睡了，因为在那次长途旅行后我非常累。我不知道第二天会发生什么。我想我已经尽力了。第二天早上，我醒来了。第二天早上，我醒来了，就好像——第二天早上我醒来了，就好像我拥有了完整——我完整的生活又回来了。

00:27:39.830 --> 00:28:24.520
<v Kevin Kelly>The next morning, I woke up, and I had my entire life again. I had my future again. There was nothing special about the day. It was another ordinary day. I was reborn into ordinariness. But what more could one ask for?
<v Kevin Kelly>Kevin Kelly：第二天早上，我醒来了，我重新拥有了完整的生活。我又有了未来。这一天没有什么特别的。这是又一个平凡的日子。我重生回到了平凡之中。但人们还能要求什么呢？

00:28:24.520 --> 00:29:51.940
<v Ira Glass>Well, Kevin Kelly is 43 now. That happened when he was 27. In his latest rebirth, he is the executive editor of Wired magazine, a glossy magazine about the future and the present. He told us that he wasn't even sure he has ever even told his parents this story, even this many years later. Anyway, he spoke with me and Paul Tough from the studios of KQED in San Francisco. This is Your Radio Playhouse on WBEZ Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass：好了，Kevin Kelly现在43岁了。那是他27岁时发生的事。在他的最新重生中，他是《连线》杂志的执行主编，这是一本关于未来和当下的光面杂志。他告诉我们，即使过了这么多年，他不确定是否向父母讲述过这个故事。无论如何，他和来自旧金山KQED工作室的Paul Tough与我进行了交谈。这里是芝加哥WBEZ的《Your Radio Playhouse》。

